@import copy from '../../components/copy.clarus'
@import docs from '../../layouts/docs.clarus'
@import callout from '../../components/callout.clarus'

<docs [title]="'Islands'" [path]="'/guide/islands'" [description]="'Interactive components that attach to server HTML, and what ships to the browser.'">
  <h1>Islands</h1>
  <p class="lead">A component with a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> is interactive: an island. It is rendered to HTML on the server like everything else, then <em>attached</em> in the browser: its event handlers and updates are wired to the HTML that is already there. Nothing re-renders.</p>
  <div class="sample"><pre><code verbatim><script>
  let count = state(0)
</script>

<button (click)="count++">Clicked {{ count }} times</button></code></pre><copy attach="interaction" /></div>

  <h2>What ships to the browser</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A small loader, only on pages that have islands.</li>
    <li>One chunk per interactive component, with just its <code>attach</code> function.</li>
    <li>Nothing for components without a script, and nothing for the page itself.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This page's text and layout ship no JavaScript. It loads two kinds of island: the theme toggle in the header, and the copy buttons on code samples, which use <code>attach="interaction"</code> so their code only loads when you click one.</p>
  <callout><p>In the HTML, an island is a <code>&lt;cl-island&gt;</code> element that names its chunk and props, and its reactive text sits between <code>&lt;!--[0--&gt;</code> markers. View the source of the home page to see it.</p></callout>
</docs>
